# [OPUS-5] sq-zgbso.2 — ODRL stateless core: stratum A0 (operand-shape guard).
#
# WHY THIS STRATUM EXISTS (fail-OPEN fix, PR #3458 review).
# Every satisfaction fact the strata derive — `odrlx:atomicSat`, `odrlx:lcSat` — is
# keyed on the constraint NODE, not on the individual operand triple. RDF puts no
# cardinality bound on a node's properties, so a single node may carry SEVERAL
# `odrl:leftOperand` / `odrl:operator` / `odrl:rightOperand` objects (and a logical
# constraint may carry several combinators). The stratum-A rule bodies then match the
# CROSS PRODUCT of those objects, and any ONE satisfiable combination marks the whole
# node satisfied — i.e. "SOME operand is satisfied" standing in for the required
# "EVERY operand is satisfied".
#
# The measured consequence: a permission constrained by `recipient eq alice` AND
# `dateTime lteq 2000-01-01` on one node GRANTED long after expiry, while the Rust
# reference evaluator (which folds an ambiguous multi-valued node into an
# unsatisfiable guard) denied.
#
# This stratum runs FIRST and marks every such node `odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous`.
# The stratum-A/C satisfaction rules negate that mark (`log:notIncludes`, stratified
# NAF over A0's COMPLETE output), so an ambiguous node is never marked satisfied and
# the quantifier direction stops mattering: satisfaction is only ever derived for a
# node carrying EXACTLY ONE operand tuple, where "some" and "every" coincide.
#
# This is the rule-layer half of the fix and is deliberately fail-CLOSED-only: it can
# suppress a grant, never create one. It cannot make the deny side safe on its own —
# suppressing an ambiguous PROHIBITION's satisfaction would DROP its deny and widen
# access — so the Rust driver additionally REFUSES the whole call for any policy
# carrying an ambiguous constraint node (`unambiguous_operands` in odrl_bridge.rs).
# Both layers are load-bearing; neither subsumes the other.
#
# Stratification (5 strata now):
#   A0 (this file): odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous — operand/combinator-arity guard
#   A  (odrl-a.n3): action facts + atomicSat + lcSat(or)   [NAF over A0]
#   B  (odrl-b.n3): andSubUnsat + anyRuleConstrUnsat + unconstrained prohibMatches
#   C  (odrl-c.n3): lcSat(and) + CONSTRAINED prohibMatches + deny emission
#   D  (odrl-d.n3): permission grant emission
#
# odrlx: predicates are sparq-internal derivation facts; never exposed in the auth
# view, and a caller-supplied policy may not mention them (validate_policy_for_n3).

@prefix odrl:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/odrl/2/> .
@prefix odrlx: <https://sparq.dev/ns/odrlx#> .
@prefix log:   <http://www.w3.org/2000/10/swap/log#> .

# ── ambiguous ATOMIC operand positions ───────────────────────────────────────
# Two DISTINCT objects in one operand position: which one does the node mean? RDF
# does not say, and there is no pairing between the positions, so the node denotes
# a cross product of operand tuples rather than one comparison.

{ ?c odrl:leftOperand ?a . ?c odrl:leftOperand ?b . ?a log:notEqualTo ?b . }
=> { ?c odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous } .

{ ?c odrl:operator ?a . ?c odrl:operator ?b . ?a log:notEqualTo ?b . }
=> { ?c odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous } .

{ ?c odrl:rightOperand ?a . ?c odrl:rightOperand ?b . ?a log:notEqualTo ?b . }
=> { ?c odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous } .

{ ?c odrl:rightOperandReference ?a . ?c odrl:rightOperandReference ?b .
  ?a log:notEqualTo ?b . }
=> { ?c odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous } .

# A node giving BOTH a literal/IRI right operand and a right-operand REFERENCE is
# ambiguous for the same reason (two candidate right values, no pairing).
{ ?c odrl:rightOperand ?a . ?c odrl:rightOperandReference ?b . }
=> { ?c odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous } .

# ── ambiguous LOGICAL-constraint combinators ─────────────────────────────────
# A node carrying two different combinators is read by the or-rule as a disjunction
# AND by the and-rule as a conjunction, so a satisfied `odrl:or` member would satisfy
# it even with an unsatisfied `odrl:and` member — the same "some for every" fail-open
# one level up. Mark it ambiguous so NEITHER lcSat rule fires.

{ ?lc odrl:or  ?x . ?lc odrl:and  ?y . } => { ?lc odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous } .
{ ?lc odrl:or  ?x . ?lc odrl:xone ?y . } => { ?lc odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous } .
{ ?lc odrl:and ?x . ?lc odrl:xone ?y . } => { ?lc odrlx:shape odrlx:Ambiguous } .
